{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.BraidedTensorDual","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.BraidedTensorDual","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.BraidedSlide","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.RigidTensorDual","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.PivotalCategory","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.Ribbon"],"declarations":14,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.pivotalOfRibbon","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.pivotalOfRibbon","kind":"instance","line":345,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/BraidedTensorDual.lean#L345-L367","doc":"**A ribbon category is pivotal** — in the literature's monoidal sense.\n\nThis replaces the `pivotalOfBraided` instance that `QOU.MathlibExt.PivotalCategory` used to carry.\nBraiding alone is not enough: `drinfeldIso` misses monoidality by the inverse monodromy.  A\nbalancing supplies exactly that monodromy (`BalancedCategory.twist_tensor`), so `θ ≫ drinfeldIso`\nis monoidal and the class is satisfied.\n\nThe classical statement behind it is that a ribbon element `v` makes `uv⁻¹` grouplike.\n\n`Ribbon.lean` makes every symmetric category balanced (`θ = 𝟙`, the double braid being trivial), so\nthis instance still fires for `FGModuleCat K` and every other symmetric rigid category — no\nlinear algebra involved, exactly as before. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.twist_drinfeld_monoidal_tensorDoubleDualIso","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.twist_drinfeld_monoidal_tensorDoubleDualIso","kind":"theorem","line":332,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/BraidedTensorDual.lean#L332-L343","doc":"**The ribbon repair, in canonical form.**\n\n`twist_drinfeld_monoidal` stated against `pivotorTensor`; this states the same thing against the\ncanonical comparison `drinfeldIso (X ⊗ Y) ≫ tensorDoubleDualIso X Y`, which is the shape a\n`PivotalCategory` monoidality field would take. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.drinfeldIso_comp_tensorDoubleDualIso","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.drinfeldIso_comp_tensorDoubleDualIso","kind":"theorem","line":295,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/BraidedTensorDual.lean#L295-L330","doc":"**The two comparisons agree** — `pivotorTensor` is the canonical one.\n\n`pivotorTensor X Y` was built by dual-uniqueness between two pairings of `Yᘁ ⊗ Xᘁ`, which is the\nconvenient form for comparing against the componentwise `drinfeldIso`s.  The *canonical* monoidal\nstructure map of the double-dual functor is instead `drinfeldIso (X ⊗ Y)` followed by\n`tensorDoubleDualIso X Y`, which routes through `(X ⊗ Y)ᘁᘁ`.  This says they are the same map, so\nthe monoidality statement below can be read either way.\n\nThe two are characterised against pairings on *different* base objects — `Yᘁ ⊗ Xᘁ` on one side,\n`(X ⊗ Y)ᘁ` on the other — so `rightDualIso_hom_unique` does not compare them directly.  The bridge\nis that `tensorRightDualIso X Y : (X ⊗ Y)ᘁ ≅ Yᘁ ⊗ Xᘁ` is itself a dual comparison, so it carries\nits own evaluation identity, and `braiding_naturality_right` moves it across the braiding that\n`drinfeldIso` contributes.  Four evaluation identities and one interchange, no diagram chase. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.twist_drinfeld_monoidal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.twist_drinfeld_monoidal","kind":"theorem","line":268,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/BraidedTensorDual.lean#L268-L293","doc":"**The ribbon repair: `θ ≫ drinfeldIso` IS monoidal.**\n\n`drinfeldIso` misses monoidality by the inverse monodromy (`pivotorTensor_hom`); the balancing\naxiom `θ_{X ⊗ Y} = β_{X,Y} ≫ β_{Y,X} ≫ (θ_X ⊗ θ_Y)` supplies exactly the monodromy, and the two\ncancel.  The step that makes the cancellation go through is `monodromy_inv_comm`: `θ_X ⊗ θ_Y`\ncommutes past the monodromy by braiding naturality, so the `β β` from the balancing axiom meets its\nown inverse.\n\nThis is the categorical form of the classical fact that a ribbon element `v` makes `uv⁻¹`\ngrouplike, and it is why **ribbon**, not merely braided, is the hypothesis under which a rigid\ncategory is pivotal in the monoidal sense. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.monodromy_inv_comm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.monodromy_inv_comm","kind":"theorem","line":261,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/BraidedTensorDual.lean#L261-L266","doc":"The monodromy commutes with a tensor of endomorphisms — braiding naturality, twice. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.pivotorTensor_hom","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.pivotorTensor_hom","kind":"theorem","line":238,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/BraidedTensorDual.lean#L238-L258","doc":"**`drinfeldIso` fails monoidality by exactly the inverse monodromy.**\n\nNot `(drinfeldIso X).hom ⊗ₘ (drinfeldIso Y).hom`, as a monoidal pivotal structure would require,\nbut that composed with `((β_ X Y).hom ≫ (β_ Y X).hom)⁻¹`.  In a symmetric category the correction\nis the identity and monoidality does hold; in a genuinely braided one it does not.\n\nThree lemmas compose: `rightDualIso_hom_trans` factors the comparison through the intermediate\ntensor-then-swap pairing, `rightDualIso_swap_tensor` evaluates the first factor as the inverse\nmonodromy, and `rightDualIso_tensorExactPairing` evaluates the second as the tensor of the two\n`drinfeldIso`s. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.pivotorTensor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.pivotorTensor","kind":"def","line":220,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/BraidedTensorDual.lean#L220-L236","doc":"**The comparison a monoidal pivotal structure must satisfy**, `X ⊗ Y ≅ Xᘁᘁ ⊗ Yᘁᘁ`.\n\nBuilt by dual-uniqueness between two pairings of `Yᘁ ⊗ Xᘁ`, which is what makes it directly\ncomparable with the componentwise `drinfeldIso`s.\n\nIt **is** the canonical comparison: `drinfeldIso_comp_tensorDoubleDualIso` below proves\n`drinfeldIso (X ⊗ Y) ≫ (tensorDoubleDualIso X Y).hom = (pivotorTensor X Y).hom`, so the\nmonoidality results here can be read against either form.\n\n*An earlier revision recorded this as open and predicted the route: transporting a pairing along\n`tensorRightDualIso X Y` via Mathlib's `exactPairingCongrLeft`, plus a `rightDualIso` compatibility\nlemma nobody had written.  **Neither was needed.**  `tensorRightDualIso` is itself a `rightDualIso`,\nso it already carries an evaluation identity, and `braiding_naturality_right` moves it across the\nbraiding `drinfeldIso` contributes.* ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.doubleDualTensorPairing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.doubleDualTensorPairing","kind":"def","line":215,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/BraidedTensorDual.lean#L215-L218","doc":"`Xᘁᘁ ⊗ Yᘁᘁ` as a right dual of `Yᘁ ⊗ Xᘁ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.tensorSwapPairing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.tensorSwapPairing","kind":"def","line":207,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/BraidedTensorDual.lean#L207-L213","doc":"Tensor-then-swap: the same objects, the other pairing.  `rightDualIso_swap_tensor` says these\ntwo differ by the inverse monodromy. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.swapTensorPairing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.swapTensorPairing","kind":"def","line":200,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/BraidedTensorDual.lean#L200-L205","doc":"Swap-then-tensor: `X ⊗ Y` as a right dual of `Yᘁ ⊗ Xᘁ`, via `exactPairing_swap` of the\ntensor pairing. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.rightDualIso_swap_tensor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.rightDualIso_swap_tensor","kind":"theorem","line":170,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/BraidedTensorDual.lean#L170-L187","doc":"**Swap-then-tensor and tensor-then-swap are compared by the inverse monodromy.**\n\nThe dual-uniqueness isomorphism between the two pairings of `Y' ⊗ X'` with `X ⊗ Y` is *not* the\nidentity: it is `((β_ X Y).hom ≫ (β_ Y X).hom)⁻¹`.  In a **symmetric** category the monodromy is\ntrivial and the two pairings agree; in a genuinely braided one they do not. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.evaluation_swap_tensor_eq_monodromy","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.evaluation_swap_tensor_eq_monodromy","kind":"theorem","line":129,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/BraidedTensorDual.lean#L129-L168","doc":"**The two pairings differ by exactly the inverse monodromy.**\n\n`X ⊗ Y` is a right dual of `Y' ⊗ X'` in two ways — swap-then-tensor (left) and tensor-then-swap\n(right) — and their evaluations differ by `((β_ X Y).hom ≫ (β_ Y X).hom)⁻¹` applied to `X ⊗ Y`.\n\nBoth pushes, assembled: `evaluation_swap_tensor_step` removes the `Y`-over-`X'` crossing, one\ninterchange separates the two evaluations (they act on disjoint strands), and\n`braiding_cap_monodromy` removes the `X`-over-`Y'` crossing and delivers the monodromy. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.braiding_cap_monodromy","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.braiding_cap_monodromy","kind":"theorem","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/BraidedTensorDual.lean#L92-L127","doc":"**The second push, and the monodromy it leaves.**\n\nThree strands: `X` crossing the closed `Y`-pair.  Pushing the `X`-over-`Y'` crossing off the\n`Y`-cap leaves the **inverse monodromy** `((β_ X Y).hom ≫ (β_ Y X).hom)⁻¹` acting on `X ⊗ Y` at\nthe top.\n\nThe proof is three moves and no diagram chase.  Write `(β_ Y X).inv = M⁻¹ ≫ (β_ X Y).hom`, which\nsplits the crossing into a monodromy and an *honest* braiding.  Recognise the two leading crossings\nas the block braiding `β_{X ⊗ Y, Y'}` (`braiding_tensor_left_hom`), so that\n`braiding_naturality_left` carries the monodromy from the middle to the top.  What is left is\nexactly `braiding_slide_cap_right` at the `Y`-cap. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.evaluation_swap_tensor_step","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.evaluation_swap_tensor_step","kind":"theorem","line":43,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/BraidedTensorDual.lean#L43-L90","doc":"**The first of the two pushes.**\n\nThe left-hand side is the evaluation of `exactPairing_swap` applied to `tensorExactPairing` — the\n\"swap-then-tensor\" pairing.  The right-hand side has the `Y`-over-`X'` crossing removed: it has been\npushed off the `X`-evaluation by `braiding_slide_cap_right_inv`, leaving `(β_ Y X).inv` behind.  That\nleftover is the first half of the monodromy.\n\nThe proof is: two hexagons to expand the block braiding, one interchange to bring the crossing next\nto the evaluation it must cross, and the inverse slide. ","signatureChunk":0}]}